# Local development credentials for @elafda/bot.
#
# Copy this file and fill in the values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Wrangler reads .env from the directory holding wrangler.jsonc, which is why
# this lives in apps/bot rather than the repository root. Use .env or .dev.vars,
# never both: defining .dev.vars makes wrangler ignore .env entirely.

# =============================================================================
# WHERE THESE COME FROM
# =============================================================================
# X developer portal, on the app owned by @eLafdaBot:
#   Projects & Apps -> your app -> "Keys and tokens"
#
# That page shows three credential groups. You need exactly one of them:
#
#   Bearer Token (App-Only)   IGNORE. App-only auth cannot post as @eLafdaBot.
#   OAuth 2.0 Keys            IGNORE. User tokens expire every ~2 hours and need
#                             refresh-token rotation, which a cron Worker would
#                             have to store durably and could be bricked by
#                             losing. SPEC.md section 19 records this choice.
#   OAuth 1.0 Keys            USE THESE. They do not expire.
#
# =============================================================================
# BEFORE YOU COPY ANYTHING: CHECK THE ACCESS TOKEN SAYS "READ AND WRITE"
# =============================================================================
# The portal prints the token's permission next to it. If it says "Read", the
# bot will authenticate successfully and then fail to post.
#
# Fixing it takes two steps, and the second is the one people miss:
#
#   1. User authentication settings -> App permissions -> "Read and write".
#      X also requires a Callback URI and Website URL on that form even though
#      the bot never logs a user in. https://elafda.org/ works for both.
#   2. Back to "Keys and tokens" -> REGENERATE the Access Token and Secret.
#
# A token issued while the app was read-only stays read-only forever. Changing
# the setting does not upgrade a token that already exists.
#
# =============================================================================
# THE VALUES
# =============================================================================

# Portal label: "API Key". Also called the consumer key.
X_BOT_API_KEY=""

# Portal label: "API Key Secret". Shown once, at creation time.
X_BOT_API_SECRET=""

# Portal label: "Access Token", under OAuth 1.0 Keys, for @eLafdaBot.
# Must read "Read and Write". Regenerate it if it does not.
X_BOT_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# Portal label: "Access Token Secret". Regenerated together with the token
# above, so always copy the pair at the same time.
X_BOT_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# The bot account's NUMERIC id, not the handle. The mentions endpoint takes an
# id. The portal does not show it. Fill the four values above, then run:
#
#     npm run verify:bot-credentials
#
# That makes one authenticated read of the bot's own account, prints the id,
# and confirms the signature is acceptable to X. It posts nothing.
#
# This value is public rather than secret. Move it into the "vars" block of
# wrangler.jsonc if you would rather keep it in version control.
X_BOT_USER_ID=""

# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO
# =============================================================================
# It does not configure a deployed Worker. Production values are set on the
# Worker itself and are never written to a file in this repository:
#
#     npx wrangler secret put X_BOT_API_KEY
#
# The names are declared in wrangler.jsonc under "secrets.required", so a
# deploy fails and names the gaps rather than shipping a Worker that cannot
# authenticate.
#
# Behaviour switches (BOT_PAUSED, BOT_REPLY_MODE, BOT_MAX_REPLIES_PER_RUN) are
# not secrets and live in the "vars" block of wrangler.jsonc instead.
